WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:05.260
The tally of death and destruction

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.420
keeps growing in the Northern
California wildfires.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.420
Thick clouds of ash are
turning day into night.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.770
2020 has been another

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.850
record setting wildfire season.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:17.950
Over the last few years,
wildfires have been raging

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.510
not just in the Western US,
but all around the world.

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.140
Fires in the Amazon rainforest

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.740
and Australia's brush fires are so massive

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.830
that they can be seen from space.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.740
And while fires have
long been a natural part

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.360
of many forest ecosystems,
higher temperatures

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.510
and longer droughts
caused by climate change

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.550
are drying out forests and
turning trees into tinder.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.163
Meaning that in areas like California

00:00:39.163 --> 00:00:42.670
wildfire seasons have become supercharged.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.130
Towns like Paradise, California,

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:48.000
where the 2018 Camp Fire
destroyed over 80% of its homes

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.300
are now grappling with how to
adapt to this new age of fire,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.330
starting from essentially the ground up.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.133
It is literally like the
surface of the moon there.

00:00:56.133 --> 00:00:58.620
Two years later, as the town still works

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.650
to rebuild, the local parks
and recreation district

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.400
is beginning an ambitious plan

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.250
to not just restore Paradise but make sure

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.833
it can better defend
itself from the next fire.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:18.820
CityLab reporter Laura Bliss
went to see this firsthand.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.101
So Laura, you just went to Paradise.

00:01:21.101 --> 00:01:24.197
Can you sort of explain what's
it like to be there now?

00:01:24.197 --> 00:01:26.800
It was a pretty haunting experience.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.710
Just thousands and thousands
of homes literally reduced

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.230
to shells, kind of gutted out foundations.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:36.963
It really still is this
landscape of disaster.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:42.260
So I guess what has the scale
of rebuilding been like?

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.180
I checked with the city

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.960
and there's been about 400 homes

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.530
that have already been reconstructed

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.840
and about 1200 that are
currently permitted.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.780
Even as this kind of instinct to rebuild

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:56.940
is sweeping over the town
and over former residents,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.680
there are some people who are thinking

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.570
a little bit differently
about what the future

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.463
of Paradise should look like.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.000
One resident I spoke to
has actually gone forward

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.730
with selling her land so that eventually

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:13.610
it might become part of a
large green belt encircling

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.600
the city to potentially protect
it from future wildfires.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.400
And the person who's leading this project

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.030
is not a member of city government.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.230
He's actually the manager

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.593
of the Paradise Recreation
and Parks District.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.130
My name is Dan Efseaff,
I'm the district manager

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.640
for the Paradise Recreation
and Park District.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.240
So we've provided everything
from swimming lessons

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.950
to people's first sports experience,

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:39.130
to hosting a variety of clubs.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.603
And we've been a fabric of
the community for decades.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.360
There were quite a few lessons
from the Camp Fire for us.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:47.880
One of them was the role

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:49.880
we potentially have as a parks district.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.050
And Dan believes what the park district

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.480
can start to do is improve.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.650
What's known as the
wildland urban interface

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.417
It's that line between full-blown nature

00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:05.320
and wildland areas, and then
the more developed space.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.450
In Paradise that line
was very much blurred

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.330
or a razor's edge.

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:11.992
So the idea here is
that we would take that

00:03:11.992 --> 00:03:16.710
very narrow razor sharp
wildland urban interface now,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:20.110
and expand it out in such a way
that we would have a buffer.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.610
At the same time, providing these kinds

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:25.710
of extraordinary recreational
resources and opportunities.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.580
Dan and the parks and recreation district

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.030
are in the very early
phases of acquiring land

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.990
in hopes of connecting them in what would

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.570
be basically a giant moat of green space.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.210
So if the nascent plan is fully realized,

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.630
thousands of acres of land could be used

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.090
for respite and play by
the residents of Paradise.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.850
Parks and baseball
fields could also double

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.600
as future fuel breaks for fire.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.460
So areas that could be parks

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.950
they could be returned in
some cases to orchards.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:57.750
We're interested in
ag-tourism in the area.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.230
So we're trying to do is take this idea

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.990
of defensible space around a house

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:05.343
and expand that to defensible
space around the community.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.561
This plan is taking
shape now as California

00:04:09.561 --> 00:04:13.540
and all of the Western US is on fire.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.492
Already this year, millions
of acres have burned,

00:04:16.492 --> 00:04:19.510
tens of thousands of people
have had to evacuate.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:21.450
With climate change
driving up temperatures

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.620
and drying out forests there's
no question that the risk

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:28.120
of catastrophe recurring in
communities like Paradise

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:30.633
and Paradise itself, is very, very real.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.770
But for Dan, he's hoping that this plan

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.710
can make Paradise not just a dire warning

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.620
of the damage wildfires can cause

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.210
but rather a model on how to
better protect communities

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:41.740
in fire prone regions.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:42.573
Earthquake!

00:04:42.573 --> 00:04:44.710
If major cities all up
and down the West Coast

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:46.730
that sit on major earthquake faults.

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.990
We've mitigated that,
we've engineered our way

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:53.000
into having less danger associated

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.530
with earthquakes in major cities.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.950
And we can do the same with fire.

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:58.574
We just haven't done it yet.

00:04:58.574 --> 00:04:59.750
This is Crystal Kolden.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.360
She's a geographer by training.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.734
I call myself a pyro geographer

00:05:04.734 --> 00:05:08.960
because almost all of my work
over the last 15, 20 years

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.710
has been across this really broad spectrum

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:16.370
of understanding how
humans and the environment

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:19.410
are affected by and interact with fire.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.370
There are a lot of different factors

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:25.140
that contribute to changing wildfires,

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.210
but climate change is the thing

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.130
that amplifies all of those changes.

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.230
So when we have a high temperature,
there's a lot more heat

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.910
and that heat translates into fires

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:37.810
being able to burn more easily

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.120
and because they're able
to burn more easily,

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:41.580
the conditions are dryer,

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.980
they're able to burn a lot
hotter and a lot faster.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:46.810
And they're much, much more difficult

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.833
for firefighters to contain.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.400
While around the country
the number of wild land

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:55.310
fires have actually been
going down since 1990.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.313
The acres burned has at times doubled.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:01.730
The idea in Paradise is to
use what's called a greenbelt

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.500
to help slow the
destructiveness of future fires.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:08.500
Green belts are a type
of fuel break, right?

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:12.330
Greenbelts are of course,
these areas where a lot

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:15.400
of the understory is removed under trees

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.160
so that they become essentially
park-like grasslands.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.190
This is one of the jewels of our area.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.480
This is Paradise Lake.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.470
This was a location where
the fire slowed down

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:27.090
and stopped on its own.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.530
You can definitely see evidence
of fire through this area,

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:33.500
but the flame lengths are
relatively low on these trees.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:37.240
Look at all of these
small trees that were cut.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.790
These were cut before the fire.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.100
So that created a more open understory

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.170
which carried the fire less
than it would have otherwise.

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:46.210
Dan wants to use the
land that they acquire

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.840
to create more areas that look like this.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.860
We are going to pursue a strategy here.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:54.040
These oaks that are sprouting
up, make them less bushy

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.790
make it less of a brush field over time

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:59.290
and encourage one particular
leader out of here

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:00.730
to grow into a tree.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:03.030
It's really important to
note that it's not gonna be

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.330
as simple as just planting
some trees and grass

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:06.910
and hoping for the best.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:10.480
Firebreaks are not invulnerable
to the spread of fires.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.750
High winds, especially
the ones that we've seen

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:15.313
igniting some of the fires
of these catastrophic events

00:07:15.313 --> 00:07:16.683
in the last couple of years

00:07:16.683 --> 00:07:19.763
can carry burning embers as far as a mile.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.930
A common misconception that people have

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:25.100
is that a fire break should
just stop a fire, right?

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.650
That's the definition, right?

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.870
It's meant to break a
fire's forward progress

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:30.270
and it should do it all by itself.

00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:31.730
And that's really a misconception

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.070
because that is not what the
goal of a firebreak usually is.

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:39.520
It is specifically
meant to be a safe place

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:42.000
for firefighters to take a stand

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.630
and try and modify a
fire's forward progress

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:47.933
as it's coming into a community.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:50.720
You know, I keep thinking
about this with a story that

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:53.350
pulling off this green
belt feels like a lot

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:55.534
for a parks and rec district to pull off.

00:07:55.534 --> 00:07:57.760
How much does something like this cost?

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.290
I think Dan's back of
the envelope calculation

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:04.443
was that this is going to
require upwards of $10 million.

00:08:04.443 --> 00:08:05.960
And then that's not even including,

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:08.940
what it's going to take to
maintain all of these lands.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.600
So just to give you a sense
of what this would entail,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.770
one of the sections that Dan is targeting

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:17.170
for this green buffer
idea is a stretch of land

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.730
that runs parallel to
the Feather River Canyon.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:23.490
It's about seven miles long
and about 4,000 acres in total,

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.310
containing hundreds of properties,

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:28.370
about 80% of that area is privately owned.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:31.070
So on top of the costs
of acquiring the land,

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:34.560
it's also just the time and resources that

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.800
a little parks districts may
be challenged to work up to.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.100
Turning these former homes into parkland

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.320
also hits on the ultra-sensitive question

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.110
of planned relocation, which is this idea

00:08:46.110 --> 00:08:48.875
of government basically
coming in to assist

00:08:48.875 --> 00:08:52.700
whole communities of people
move to an area that's safer

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:54.780
from a climate change perspective.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:56.710
And that's a really difficult question

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.580
in a community that's already
been deeply traumatized

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:01.870
by this catastrophic experience.

00:09:03.235 --> 00:09:06.234
We're absolutely of the mind
we have to be respectful

00:09:06.234 --> 00:09:09.180
of all our decisions by landowners.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:10.280
I think the idea is right now

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:11.970
that there is such a receptivity

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:15.630
to people wanting to do some
things that help the community.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:17.260
Even if they choose to rebuild,

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.160
then maybe they will provide an easement

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.580
or permission to have that trail that goes

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:22.650
through the bottom of their property

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.470
that allows for fuels reduction,

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:25.710
It protects their property

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.470
in exchange for a little
bit of recreational value.

00:09:28.470 --> 00:09:30.800
I think that idea is pretty persuasive

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:32.920
for a lot of people in our community.

00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:35.020
Another issue Dan faces is that while

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.210
there's a lot of anecdotal
evidence that greenbelts can help

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:40.910
there actually isn't that
much scientific evidence

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.280
that confirms their effectiveness.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.920
One conservationist I
talked to for this story

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.150
referred to green buffers

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:49.470
as this funny combination of common sense

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.050
with not a lot of scientific backup,

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.450
but experts say that the
signs are definitely out there

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:55.740
that it can work.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:00.140
It's something that is
not really making it

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:01.570
into the scientific literature

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:03.490
because it's been relatively rare.

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:07.470
We don't have this rigorous
reproducible scientific process

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:11.190
that is often favored
by scientific journals.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:14.130
But the anecdotal data are
very much supportive of this.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:16.570
I actually published a study last year

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:19.440
about a series of firebreaks

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.690
and what was essentially a green belt

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:24.730
around the community of
Montecito California.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:28.430
Between the years of 1999 and 2017

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:31.995
Montecito would spend over $1.6 million

00:10:31.995 --> 00:10:35.320
on wildfire vulnerability
reduction activities.

00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:38.070
And over years they did
it little by little.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.820
It's individual properties,
it's along roads

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:42.633
where they have space to work.

00:10:42.633 --> 00:10:44.920
And when we actually went back

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:46.600
and looked at the satellite data

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.150
it looks a little bit
like a green necklace.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:51.025
And in 2017, the Thomas fire

00:10:51.025 --> 00:10:53.370
would put that work to the test.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.835
The calmer conditions in
Santa Barbara County Wednesday

00:10:55.835 --> 00:10:58.040
didn't mean flames stopped threatening

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.470
multimillion dollar homes in Montecito.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:04.310
This is the fifth largest
fire in California history.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:05.970
And when that fire hit Montecito

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.180
there were winds blowing
over 60 miles an hour.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:11.360
There were gusts up to 72 miles an hour.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.530
And that was the last
thing we had recorded

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:15.340
before the weather station burned down.

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:18.470
And when it got to that green belt

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.450
along the edge of the town,
it basically did exactly

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:23.110
what the Greenbelt was designed to do,

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:26.016
which is it died down,
it allowed firefighters

00:11:26.016 --> 00:11:30.270
to move in close and
work safely to put water

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:33.070
on the edge of the fire
to actually drop it down

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:35.580
to the ground and put it out.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:38.820
And it is one of the
few cases that is really

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:41.790
well-documented
retrospectively and spatially

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.730
of a greenbelt working well.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:46.883
We're trying to find more of
those, but it's a challenge.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:51.456
What we're talking about is an idea

00:11:51.456 --> 00:11:54.150
how do we live with
fire on this landscape?

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.050
Potentially we could serve a model

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:58.480
on a whole bunch of different levels,

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:01.090
not just the parks and how
to protect the community

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:02.990
but how do we take care of our people?

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:05.330
We can do all the science we want

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:07.690
but if people don't see
how it applies to them

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.530
and how it can translate into an action

00:12:09.530 --> 00:12:11.650
that they can actually
take, then the science

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:15.110
doesn't actually become applied
in any way, shape or form.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:17.720
And we don't change actions on the ground.

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.110
There's been a lot of interest

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.390
in how do we protect Paradise.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:22.570
There's lessons here.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.480
We're far enough along
that we can demonstrate

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.750
some of those lessons to
those unfortunate communities

00:12:26.750 --> 00:12:29.110
that have suffered very
similar situations.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:32.065
But also there's models
